cli-cmd-workflow-new =
    .about = Record a local workflow from a definition file (`-` reads stdin)
cli-cmd-workflow-promote =
    .about = Copy a goal's design into the library
cli-cmd-workflow-restart =
    .about = Restart every run of the workspace of the workflow that is going
cli-cmd-workflow-rm =
    .about = Forget a workflow. Refused while a goal or another workflow uses it
cli-cmd-workflow-run =
    .about = Run a workflow in the workspace — no goal — and follow the run; with `--start` naming an event start, a test run
cli-cmd-workflow-runs =
    .about = The workflow's runs of the workspace, newest first
cli-cmd-workflow-show =
    .about = Show one workflow — by id, or by catalog slug — with its problems
cli-cmd-workflow-stop =
    .about = Stop every run of the workspace of the workflow that is going
cli-cmd-workflow-use =
    .about = Point a goal at the workflow its next run will use
cli-cmd-workflow-validate =
    .about = Every problem a definition has, without recording it
cli-cmd-workspace =
    .about = People, invitations and the workspaces you are a guest of
cli-cmd-workspace-hosts =
    .about = The workspaces you are a guest of
cli-cmd-workspace-invite =
    .about = Make a single-use invitation: prints the link and the code
cli-cmd-workspace-invites =
    .about = Every invitation and where it stands
cli-cmd-workspace-join =
    .about = Join a workspace from an invitation link or code
cli-cmd-workspace-leave =
    .about = Leave a workspace you are a guest of
cli-cmd-workspace-people =
    .about = Everyone in this workspace: the owner and the people hosted here
cli-cmd-workspace-reindex =
    .about = Throw the index away and rebuild it from the truth files — for a cache you have reason to doubt. Refused while a node holds the workspace
cli-cmd-workspace-check =
    .about = Read every file of the workspace as the next open would, without opening it: what a crash tore, and what earlier opens moved aside. Exit 1 while anything is found
cli-cmd-workspace-remove =
    .about = Remove a person. What they already received cannot be un-sent
cli-cmd-workspace-revoke =
    .about = Withdraw a pending invitation
cli-cmd-workspace-role =
    .about = Change a person's role: admin | member | guest
cli-cmd-workstream =
    .about = Workstreams: a branch and a checkout where one piece of work happens
cli-cmd-workstream-close =
    .about = Close a workstream. The checkout stays on disk unless `--tree`
cli-cmd-workstream-commit =
    .about = Stage everything and commit
cli-cmd-workstream-diff =
    .about = The patch a single commit would record right now
cli-cmd-workstream-list =
    .about = List workstreams, optionally filtered
cli-cmd-workstream-open =
    .about = Open a checkout on its own branch
cli-cmd-workstream-pr =
    .about = Open a pull request on the code host behind `origin` — through the Publish gate. A branch not on the remote yet is pushed first, under the same gate
cli-cmd-workstream-pr-checks =
    .about = Check runs on the workstream's pull request
cli-cmd-workstream-pr-merge =
    .about = Merge the workstream's pull request — through the Publish gate
cli-cmd-workstream-pr-review =
    .about = Review the workstream's pull request
cli-cmd-workstream-pr-view =
    .about = The workstream's pull request, read fresh from the code host
cli-cmd-workstream-push =
    .about = Push the branch to origin — through the Publish gate
cli-cmd-workstream-show =
    .about = Show a workstream: branch, distance from base, changed files

## The help of the verbs of listening, signals and test runs.
cli-arg-run-data =
    .help = A test run's sample event as JSON: what the start's mapping reads, as if it had happened
cli-arg-run-start =
    .help = The start step a run begins at: the start by hand is a run now, an event start a test run
cli-arg-signal-emit-goal =
    .help = Raise it on this goal rather than in the workspace
cli-arg-signal-emit-name =
    .help = The signal's name: dot-separated lowercase parts, e.g. `report.ready`
cli-arg-signal-list-limit =
    .help = How many to list, newest first
cli-arg-workflow-hook-secret-host =
    .help = Whose hook it is: `workflow` or `goal`
cli-arg-workflow-hook-secret-id =
    .help = The workflow's id, or the goal's
cli-arg-workflow-hook-secret-rotate =
    .help = Mint a new secret: the old one stops verifying at once
cli-arg-workflow-hook-secret-step =
    .help = The hook start's step id
cli-arg-workflow-listeners-goal =
    .help = A goal's listeners instead
cli-arg-workflow-listeners-id =
    .help = A workflow's id; without it and without `--goal`, every listener
cli-arg-workflow-on-budget_secs =
    .help = Wall-clock ceiling in seconds for each run its events start; 0 lifts it
cli-arg-workflow-on-budget_tokens =
    .help = Token ceiling for each run its events start; 0 lifts it
cli-arg-workflow-on-budget_usd_cents =
    .help = Cost ceiling in US cents for each run its events start; 0 lifts it
cli-arg-workflow-on-inputs =
    .help = An input its event runs bind, `name=value` (repeatable): what its start events read and do not map
cli-arg-workflow-run-data =
    .help = A test run's sample event as JSON: what the start's mapping reads, as if it had happened
cli-arg-workflow-run-start =
    .help = The start step the run begins at: the start by hand, or — for a test run — an event start
cli-cmd-signal-release =
    .about = Let a held signal through: an outside payload the content screen would not pass, read by a person
cli-cmd-workflow-hook-secret =
    .about = A hook start's standing — its paths, whether its secret is minted — or, with `--rotate`, a new secret, shown once
cli-cmd-workflow-listeners =
    .about = The listeners — every start event armed for a workflow that is On or a goal that listens: one workflow's, one goal's, or all
cli-cmd-workflow-off =
    .about = Turn a workflow Off: its start events are no longer heard; a run already going goes on
cli-cmd-workflow-on =
    .about = Turn a workflow On: its start events are heard from now on, each occurrence a run of the workspace. Prints what it listens for, and each public hook's secret once

